("p", "Because atoms cannot be created or destroyed, the number of atoms of each element must be the same on both sides. An equation that satisfies this is a **balanced chemical equation**. Mg + O<sub>2</sub> &rarr; MgO has 2 oxygen atoms on the left and 1 on the right, so it is unbalanced."),
("h2", "Balancing Equations: the Hit-and-Trial Method"),
("steps", [("Step 1 — Write the skeletal equation", "Fe + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; Fe<sub>3</sub>O<sub>4</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>"), ("Step 2 — List the atoms on each side", "Left: Fe 1, H 2, O 1. Right: Fe 3, H 2, O 4. Begin with the compound that has the most atoms, Fe<sub>3</sub>O<sub>4</sub>, and balance an element that appears in it."), ("Step 3 — Balance oxygen, then iron, then hydrogen", "Put 4 before H<sub>2</sub>O to get 4 oxygen atoms. Hydrogen on the left is now 8, so put 4 before H<sub>2</sub>. Put 3 before Fe to match three Fe atoms."), ("Step 4 — Check and add state symbols", "3Fe(s) + 4H<sub>2</sub>O(g) &rarr; Fe<sub>3</sub>O<sub>4</sub>(s) + 4H<sub>2</sub>(g). Count each element again on both sides.")]),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "While balancing, change only the **coefficients** (the numbers in front of formulae). Never change the **subscripts**: that would change the substance. For instance H<sub>2</sub>O and H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> are different compounds."),
